cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite it as below."
title: "O*NET Agentic Coverage Dataset"
abstract: >-
  A task-level estimate, for all 18,796 O*NET work tasks (the full O*NET task
  corpus, economy-wide), of how much of each task a current autonomous AI agent
  could complete end to end with no human in the loop. Labels were generated
  with a large language model (Claude Sonnet) against a single neutral
  instrument, applied in one consistent pass across the full corpus, then
  validated: three independent human annotators each scored all 200 tasks of an
  audit sample, giving Spearman rho = 0.884 against the model (99.9% of the
  0.885 reliability ceiling set by inter-annotator agreement), with a documented
  mean calibration bias of -0.147 (model conservative relative to humans in
  absolute level; ranking is not affected). Built as a companion resource to the
  ATES (Agentic Task Exposure Score) framework and the O*NET Task-to-Ability
  Mapping Dataset, it also stands alone for research on AI exposure and
  human-AI task allocation.
